Claude CD shopping

There's no fixed leaderboard here — Claude doesn't hold a ranking of banks. What a CD query returns depends on whether search is on, and if it is, on which roundup pages get retrieved that day. So the useful question is *how likely is each brand to appear, and how high*, and that's what I've ranked below using what's actually live right now.

Ranking for a search-enabled "best CD rates" query (Sept 2026)

| # | Bank | Why it lands there |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Synchrony | Strongest position by far. Forbes Advisor calls it the best nine-month, one-year and five-year CD available, and Bankrate scores it 4.8/5 with a top-picks slot at 0.25%–4.35% APY. Near-certain to appear in any answer. |
| 2 | Ally | Forbes has Ally's six-month CD as the best six-month CD currently available, citing the competitive yield, no minimum and 10-day rate guarantee. Present in essentially every roundup. |
| 3 | Newtek | The rate-driven winner: NerdWallet lists Newtek at 4.30% APY on the one-year with a $2,500 minimum, and rates it 5.0 overall, noting it won NerdWallet's 2026 best savings award. Ranks #1 on pure "highest 1-year rate" phrasings. |
| 4 | Capital One | Ubiquitous in research lists and brand recall; rates aren't top-tier, so it appears as a mention rather than a pick. |
| 5 | CIT | Consistently in roundups; NerdWallet flags CIT Platinum Savings at up to 4.10% — visibility skews to savings rather than CDs. |
| 6 | Amex | Strong brand, weak product. Its one-year CD sits at 3.25% APY with a 270-day early withdrawal penalty — well below the pack. |
| 7 | Barclays | Appears in the institution lists at Bankrate, Forbes and Motley Fool, but I found it in no 2026 top-picks table. Its differentiator in the coverage that does exist is no minimum opening deposit. Present but never featured. |
| 8 | Axos | Visibility is real but sits on the savings side: NerdWallet's top HYSA rate right now is Axos at up to 4.21% with linked-checking requirements. CDs rarely surface. |
| 9 | Vio | Occasional Forbes/Fortune appearances, mostly for its 4.00% APY savings on all balances with a $100 minimum. Thin CD presence. |
| 10 | SoFi | High brand salience, low CD salience — shows up for checking/savings combos, not certificates. |
| 11 | Forbright | Savings at 3.85% and CDs topping out around 3.75% on nine months — currently uncompetitive, so it drops out of rate-sorted lists. |
| 12 | Wealthfront | Not a bank — FDIC coverage comes through partner banks, and it doesn't sell CDs. Appears only in cash/savings comparisons. |
| 13 | LendingClub | The brand is gone from CD coverage. Bankrate now lists it as Happen Bank, formerly LendingClub. Queries using the old name will increasingly miss. |
